# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here verse by verse, in the order Jesus tells it. Nothing is rearranged, and nothing from the explanation in 13:37–43 is placed into the story. The story is read first as a story.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The sowing (13:24). “The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field.” The comparison opens with a man, good seed and his own field. The seed is called good, and the field is his.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The enemy’s work in the night (13:25). “But while men slept, his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat, and went his way.” Three things happen in this verse: men sleep, an enemy comes and sows tares among the wheat, and the enemy leaves. The enemy’s work is secret and finished. The verse does not say that anyone saw it.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The tares appear (13:26). “But when the blade was sprung up, and brought forth fruit, then appeared the tares also.” The wheat grows first, and the tares are seen only when the grain has formed. Until then the difference was not plain.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The servants’ question and the householder’s answer (13:27–29). The servants of the householder come with a question: “Sir, didst not thou sow good seed in thy field? from whence then hath it tares?” (13:27). The householder answers in five words, “An enemy hath done this” (13:28). The servants ask what they will do about it: “Wilt thou then that we go and gather them up?” (13:28). He says, “Nay; lest while ye gather up the tares, ye root up also the wheat with them” (13:29). The householder does not deny that the tares are in the field, and he does not blame the seed. He names the cause, and he refuses the servants’ proposal and gives his reason.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The householder’s instruction (13:30). “Let both grow together until the harvest: and in the time of harvest I will say to the reapers, Gather ye together first the tares, and bind them in bundles to burn them: but gather the wheat into my barn.” The instruction has two parts. Until harvest both grow together. At harvest the householder himself gives the order to the reapers, and the order has a sequence: the tares are gathered first and bound in bundles to burn, and the wheat is gathered into the barn.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The turning points. The story turns three times: when the tares appear (13:26), when the servants offer to remove them and are told “Nay” (13:29), and when the householder fixes the time and the order of the separation (13:30). The narrative logic is a movement from sowing to a delayed, ordered harvest, with an enemy’s act in between and a refusal to separate early.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:24', '“sowed good seed in his field”', 'The man sows. The seed is good. The field is his.'],
    ['Matt 13:25', '“while men slept, his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat”', 'An enemy acts at night and leaves.'],
    ['Matt 13:26', '“then appeared the tares also”', 'The tares are seen when the fruit forms.'],
    ['Matt 13:27–28', '“An enemy hath done this”', 'The householder names the cause in five words.'],
    ['Matt 13:28–29', '“Nay; lest while ye gather up the tares, ye root up also the wheat with them”', 'The servants’ offer is refused, and the reason is given.'],
    ['Matt 13:30', '“Let both grow together until the harvest”', 'The tares and the wheat grow side by side to harvest.'],
    ['Matt 13:30', '“Gather ye together first the tares, and bind them in bundles to burn them: but gather the wheat into my barn”', 'The order: tares first, burned; wheat gathered into the barn.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.7 What the story leaves unsaid. Jesus does not say who the men were who slept, and He does not say whether they are the servants. He does not say how long the harvest is delayed, what the bundles are, or what the barn is. The story gives the contrast between the burned tares and the gathered wheat, and it ends there.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus names. In the house, the disciples ask for the explanation of “the parable of the tares of the field” (13:36), and Jesus answers in His own words (13:37–39). Each identification is given below as He gives it, and no other is added.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the story', 'What Jesus says it is', 'Verse'],
    ['He that soweth the good seed', '“the Son of man”', 'Matt 13:37'],
    ['The field', '“the world”', 'Matt 13:38'],
    ['The good seed', '“the children of the kingdom”', 'Matt 13:38'],
    ['The tares', '“the children of the wicked one”', 'Matt 13:38'],
    ['The enemy that sowed them', '“the devil”', 'Matt 13:39'],
    ['The harvest', '“the end of the world”', 'Matt 13:39'],
    ['The reapers', '“the angels”', 'Matt 13:39'],
], [0.34, 0.46, 0.20]))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus says will happen. He compares the burning of the tares in the story with the end: “As therefore the tares are gathered and burned in the fire; so shall it be in the end of this world” (13:40). Then He says what the Son of man will do: “The Son of man shall send forth his angels, and they shall gather out of his kingdom all things that offend, and them which do iniquity; And shall cast them into a furnace of fire: there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth” (13:41–42). And He closes, “Then shall the righteous shine forth as the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Who hath ears to hear, let him hear” (13:43).'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name. Jesus does not identify the men who slept. Jesus does not identify the servants of the householder. Jesus does not identify the barn. Jesus does not identify the bundles. Jesus does not identify the blade or the fruit. The story speaks of “a man which sowed good seed in his field” (13:24) and of “the householder” whose field it is (13:27). The explanation names the sower as the Son of man (13:37), and Jesus does not separately name the householder.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'The seven identifications in Matthew 13:37–39 are the only ones Jesus gives. A reader who adds to the list, saying that the servants are this group or the barn is that place or the men who slept are certain persons, is supplying what the Lord did not. The parable is read with the seven and no more.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The explanation does not extend to other parables. The identifications are given for this parable. They are not given for the Sower (where Jesus gives a different set of explanations in 13:18–23), and they are not given for the parables that follow in the same chapter. The field of 13:38 is the field of this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states what the comparison itself says about the Kingdom. It uses the parable’s own words, and it assigns no identity beyond those Jesus gave in Section 4.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus says, “The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field” (13:24). The comparison is a whole situation: a good sowing, an enemy’s intrusion, tares that appear when the fruit forms, a refusal to separate early, and a separation at harvest by the householder’s order. The Kingdom is likened to that situation and not to a single object in it.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the Kingdom.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The good sowing was done in the householder’s own field.** The seed is called good (13:24), and the servants speak of the field as his (13:27). The tares are not there because the seed was poor, and the householder says so: “An enemy hath done this” (13:28).',
    '**The harmful growth has an enemy’s origin.** The tares are sown “while men slept” (13:25), and the one who sows them “went his way.” The source of the trouble is another’s work, not the householder’s.',
    '**For a time, good and harmful growth stand together.** “Let both grow together until the harvest” (13:30). The Kingdom, as this comparison tells it, is not shown as a field with nothing but wheat in it before the harvest comes.',
    '**Separation before the harvest is refused.** “Lest while ye gather up the tares, ye root up also the wheat with them” (13:29). The refusal is given for a reason that concerns the wheat’s safety.',
    '**The separation itself is certain and ordered.** At harvest the householder gives the order, the tares are gathered first and burned, and the wheat is gathered into the barn (13:30). The delay does not cancel the separation.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers were people who had been told that the Kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17; 10:7). They were meant to understand that the Kingdom is not told here as a harvest already in the barn. Its good sowing is under way, an enemy’s work is already in the field, and the separation they might wish for at once waits for a time the householder fixes. They were also meant to understand that the separation, when it comes, is certain, ordered and not theirs to hurry.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the parable. The force is that of a restraint and a promise held together. The restraint is on the servants’ eagerness to remove what looks wrong. The promise is that nothing is left unjudged and that the good is gathered. It is a warning against premature separation and a warning that the separation will come.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. The Kingdom’s harvest is yet to come when the sowing is already under way. Harm in the field does not disprove the goodness of the sowing. Early separation does damage. Late separation is certain. These statements arise from the comparison itself, and they do not say that any group or object “represents” another beyond what Jesus said in 13:37–39.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say who the servants, the sleepers or the barn are, because Jesus does not identify them. It does not draw a meaning from another parable into this one. It does not treat the burning and the gathering as a way of becoming saved or lost, and it does not make the parable a picture of any group in the Body of Christ.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'Matthew 13 gathers parables that look alike, and a reader moves easily from one to another. Each is kept in its own place here. A meaning given in one is not carried into another unless Jesus carries it.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:3–9, 18–23', '“a sower went forth to sow”; “the word of the kingdom”', 'The Sower and the Soils. Four soils, one sowing, no enemy’s sowing among the wheat. Jesus’ explanation speaks of the one who hears the word, and gives a different explanation from 13:37–39.'],
    ['Mark 4:26–29', '“cast seed into the ground”; “he knoweth not how”', 'The Growing Seed. The seed grows of itself, there is no enemy and no tares, and the harvest follows when the fruit is ready.'],
    ['Matt 13:31–33', '“a grain of mustard seed”; “leaven”', 'The Mustard Seed and the Leaven. A seed becomes a tree and leaven works through meal. No enemy, no delay for the sake of the wheat, no separation, and no explanation from Jesus.'],
    ['Matt 13:44–46', '“treasure hid in a field”; “one pearl of great price”', 'The Hidden Treasure and the Pearl. A field appears here too, but the field of 13:44 is not named by Jesus. The field of 13:38 is not carried into it.'],
    ['Matt 13:47–50', '“a net, that was cast into the sea”', 'The Dragnet. A net gathers every kind, and a separation follows at the end. It shares the furnace and the wailing with 13:42, but it has no enemy, no waiting and no servants.'],
], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The Sower, the Mustard Seed, the Hidden Treasure, the Dragnet and this parable are all in Matthew 13. The Growing Seed is in Mark 4. The shared chapter does not make them one parable. This parable alone has its explanation given in the house after the multitude has gone (13:36).'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. In this parable, there is a man who sows, an enemy, servants, a householder and reapers (the reapers are named in the householder’s instruction, 13:30). In the Sower, there is a sower and four soils. In the Mustard Seed and the Leaven, a man takes a seed and a woman takes leaven. In the Dragnet, there is a net and those who draw it. The enemy and the servants belong to this parable alone.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. In this parable, the movement is sowing, an enemy’s sowing, the appearance of the tares, a refusal to separate and a harvest with an order. In the Dragnet, the movement is casting, filling, drawing to shore and sorting. The Dragnet does not include a waiting, and this parable does not include a net or a sea.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the field of 13:38 is “the world” only in this parable, and the field of 13:31 and the field of 13:44 are not explained. Second, the seed of this parable is “the children of the kingdom” (13:38), while the seed of the Sower is said to be received by the one who hears “the word of the kingdom” (13:19–23). The two explanations are not made into one. Third, “the wicked one” in 13:19 and “the devil” in 13:39 are each reported where they stand, and “the children of the wicked one” in 13:38 belongs to this parable, and neither parable’s explanation is placed into the other.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks to the multitude on the shore and to His disciples in the house, and He speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached the Kingdom of heaven as at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). The parable belongs to that ministry. The Kingdom it speaks of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. After the Gospels, the Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present in the field.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The “mysteries of the kingdom of heaven” that Jesus says are given to the disciples (13:11) are His own Kingdom matters, given to them during His earthly ministry. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul. The word is shared and the subject is not.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The field, the good seed, the tares, the harvest, the barn and “the kingdom of their Father” belong to the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel. They are not made the identity or the salvation of the Body of Christ. The Body is not called the wheat in this parable, and the Body is not placed in the field. The parable’s burning and gathering are not read as the way anyone is saved. The “children of the kingdom” of 13:38 are not made the Body’s identity.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from the way the householder acts and from the servants’ question. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Patience in judgment.** The householder tells his servants not to gather the tares now. A reader learns to hold back from rooting out what looks wrong, because the judgment is not the servants’ to carry out.',
    '**Humility about what is seen.** The tares and the wheat grow together, and the difference appears when the fruit forms. A reader learns that outward appearances before the fruit may not decide a matter, and that hurried verdicts do damage.',
    '**Trust that the final separation is the Lord’s.** The householder gives the order at harvest. A reader learns not to take for himself what the parable keeps for the householder and his reapers.',
    '**Honesty about the source of trouble.** “An enemy hath done this.” A reader learns not to assume the sowing was at fault when the householder himself names another cause.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The parable’s harvest, its barn and its kingdom of their Father are not read as the Body’s destiny, and Israel’s warnings in the parable are not turned into the Body’s identity. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells a story of a man who sowed good seed in his field, an enemy who sowed tares among the wheat while men slept, tares that appeared when the fruit formed, servants who asked to gather them and were told “Nay,” and a harvest at which the tares are bound and burned and the wheat is gathered into the barn. In the house He names seven things: the sower, the field, the good seed, the tares, the enemy, the harvest and the reapers. Everything else in the story He leaves unnamed. The comparison says that the Kingdom He announced has a good sowing, an enemy’s work in the same field, a delay that protects the wheat and a harvest in which the separation is certain. He told it during His earthly Kingdom ministry, before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that adds a referent to the servants, the barn or the sleepers, or that makes the field the Body, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** good sowing, an enemy’s sowing, the tares appear, the servants’ offer is refused, the harvest has an order.',
    '**The meaning:** good and harmful growth stand together until harvest; early separation harms the wheat; the separation at harvest is certain and ordered.',
    '**The boundaries:** only the seven identifications Jesus gives; no symbol carried in from another parable; no group assigned to the unnamed parts; the Body is not the field or the wheat.',
    '**The present lesson:** patience, humility, trust in the Lord’s separation and honesty about an enemy’s work, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

